WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.129
Welcome to our Secondary Education Online
professional learning series. Across four

2
00:00:10.129 --> 00:00:14.632
sessions, we'll explore Autistic
experience and what it means for access,

3
00:00:14.632 --> 00:00:19.573
belonging and partnership in a secondary
school. Keep the students you work with

4
00:00:19.573 --> 00:00:23.952
at the centre of the learning. We'll begin
with how we understand their

5
00:00:23.952 --> 00:00:28.455
experience, then look at communication and
the school day. We'll use that

6
00:00:28.455 --> 00:00:33.083
understanding to design a practical change
and plan a conversation with the

7
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:38.024
people who can help us refine it. This is
a recorded simulation of the workshop.

8
00:00:38.024 --> 00:00:42.465
I'll lead you through the slides,
introduce the supplied films, and give

9
00:00:42.465 --> 00:00:46.906
you time to begin each activity. The
pauses are brief. Whenever you want

10
00:00:46.906 --> 00:00:51.785
longer, pause the video and return when
you're ready. If you're watching with a

11
00:00:51.785 --> 00:00:56.601
live online group, you're welcome to use
its chat. Your own private notes work

12
00:00:56.601 --> 00:01:01.417
just as well. You don't need to come with
all the answers. Curiosity is a good

13
00:01:01.417 --> 00:01:02.352
place to begin.

14
00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:08.621
I acknowledge the Traditional Custodians
of the lands and waters from which we are

15
00:01:08.622 --> 00:01:12.777
joining this learning, and pay my respects
to Elders past and present. I

16
00:01:12.778 --> 00:01:17.226
recognise the continuing connections of
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander

17
00:01:17.227 --> 00:01:21.090
peoples to Country, culture and community.
I extend that respect to

18
00:01:21.091 --> 00:01:25.656
Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander
people taking part today. If you know the

19
00:01:25.657 --> 00:01:30.163
Country you are on, you might take a
moment to acknowledge it in your own way.

20
00:01:33.201 --> 00:01:37.593
First Nations people are advised that this
workshop may contain images and

21
00:01:37.594 --> 00:01:40.119
voices of people who are no longer with
us.

22
00:01:43.121 --> 00:01:47.914
Let's make this a workable space for
learning. Cameras are optional when you

23
00:01:47.915 --> 00:01:52.963
join others online. Listening, writing a
private note, speaking, using chat or a

24
00:01:52.964 --> 00:01:57.821
reaction, and choosing to pass are all
valid ways to take part. You can move,

25
00:01:57.822 --> 00:02:02.614
stretch, look away from the screen, or
step away and come back. The original

26
00:02:02.615 --> 00:02:07.600
workshop includes some small breakout
activities. In this recording, I'll offer

27
00:02:07.601 --> 00:02:12.266
quiet individual reflection as the
starting point. If you're watching with

28
00:02:12.267 --> 00:02:16.995
colleagues, you can pause and talk
together if you choose. You don't need a

29
00:02:16.996 --> 00:02:22.109
breakout room to complete the learning.
When you refer to a student, use initials

30
00:02:22.110 --> 00:02:27.222
or a role such as a Year 9 student. Leave
out names, school details and incidents

31
00:02:27.223 --> 00:02:31.440
that could identify someone. Keep personal
reflections private. Any

32
00:02:31.441 --> 00:02:36.106
contribution to a live group's chat should
be something you're comfortable

33
00:02:36.107 --> 00:02:41.347
sharing with that group. Take a moment to
settle on one hope for these sessions. It

34
00:02:41.348 --> 00:02:43.072
can stay in your own notes.

35
00:02:46.081 --> 00:02:51.180
Open page four of your Participant Guide,
Think about your students. Bring two or

36
00:02:51.181 --> 00:02:56.344
three students to mind, ideally students
who are quite different from one another.

37
00:02:56.345 --> 00:03:00.807
They might differ in year level,
communication, subjects they enjoy, or

38
00:03:00.808 --> 00:03:05.333
what a school day feels like for them. For
each student, note strengths,

39
00:03:05.334 --> 00:03:10.306
interests or moments of connection you
want to keep visible. Then ask yourself:

40
00:03:10.307 --> 00:03:15.470
What am I here for? What would I like to
understand better? A question is a useful

41
00:03:15.471 --> 00:03:20.506
starting point. You don't have to explain
the student or solve anything yet. Use

42
00:03:20.507 --> 00:03:25.542
Student A, B and C, or initials that only
mean something to you. This reflection

43
00:03:25.543 --> 00:03:30.451
stays private. We'll return to the same
students through the four sessions, so

44
00:03:30.452 --> 00:03:35.360
keep these notes somewhere you can find
them again. Pause here if you'd like a

45
00:03:35.361 --> 00:03:36.699
few minutes to write.

46
00:03:44.721 --> 00:03:49.731
As you come back, look at whether you've
kept the whole person in view. Alongside

47
00:03:49.732 --> 00:03:54.242
a concern or a question, is there
something they enjoy, know a great deal

48
00:03:54.243 --> 00:03:59.253
about, or do with real interest? If that
part of your picture is thin, leave room

49
00:03:59.254 --> 00:04:03.888
to find out. These notes are a starting
point for learning, and we can keep

50
00:04:03.889 --> 00:04:04.828
adding to them.

51
00:04:06.361 --> 00:04:10.869
We're going to listen to Autistic young
people in their own words. Give the

52
00:04:10.870 --> 00:04:15.865
speakers your attention without trying to
diagnose, compare them with a student, or

53
00:04:15.866 --> 00:04:20.373
pull out one lesson that stands for
everyone. Each person's account belongs

54
00:04:20.374 --> 00:04:24.943
to them. As you watch, notice what stays
with you and what you would like to

55
00:04:24.944 --> 00:04:29.208
understand more deeply. We'll return to
those questions after the film.

56
00:06:51.601 --> 00:06:55.930
Take a quiet moment with what you've
heard. On page four of your guide,

57
00:06:55.931 --> 00:07:00.941
underneath your student reflection, note
what stayed with you. What did you notice

58
00:07:00.942 --> 00:07:05.890
in the speakers' own accounts? What would
you like to understand more deeply? Try

59
00:07:05.891 --> 00:07:10.715
to keep the observation close to what
someone actually said. We can be quick to

60
00:07:10.716 --> 00:07:15.664
fill in a person's experience from their
expression or behaviour. Here, let their

61
00:07:15.665 --> 00:07:20.490
words lead. If you're with a live online
group, you may share a word or a short

62
00:07:20.491 --> 00:07:25.315
phrase in the chat. Passing is welcome.
Pause the video for more thinking time.

63
00:07:33.320 --> 00:07:37.991
Keep any questions that are still open.
You don't need to settle on one shared

64
00:07:37.992 --> 00:07:41.874
interpretation of the film. We're building
a broader picture, and

65
00:07:41.875 --> 00:07:46.668
individual accounts may show us different
things. The sensory and thinking ideas

66
00:07:46.669 --> 00:07:51.158
that follow are ways to ask better
questions. In Session Two, we'll connect

67
00:07:51.159 --> 00:07:53.706
them with communication and the school
day.

68
00:07:55.240 --> 00:07:59.948
Our starting point is neurodiversity
affirming. People experience and interact

69
00:07:59.949 --> 00:08:04.229
with the world differently. Different ways
of knowing, doing, being and

70
00:08:04.230 --> 00:08:09.243
belonging deserve recognition. Difference
does not make someone a lesser version of

71
00:08:09.244 --> 00:08:13.830
another person. That means we include
strengths, interests and joy alongside

72
00:08:13.831 --> 00:08:18.417
barriers and support needs. Recognising a
student's strengths doesn't make a

73
00:08:18.418 --> 00:08:23.126
difficult day disappear. Someone can bring
deep knowledge, care and creativity

74
00:08:23.127 --> 00:08:27.712
and still need substantial support. Think
of the students on your page. What

75
00:08:27.713 --> 00:08:32.177
becomes visible when you include what they
enjoy and how they connect? How

76
00:08:32.178 --> 00:08:36.824
might that change the language you use
about them? Instead of a label such as

77
00:08:36.825 --> 00:08:41.044
difficult, we can describe what the
environment asks, what the student

78
00:08:41.045 --> 00:08:45.203
communicates, and what support might help.
We can be specific about a

79
00:08:45.204 --> 00:08:48.505
difficulty while respecting the person
experiencing it.

80
00:08:50.040 --> 00:08:54.072
The social model gives us an
environment-first lens. We look for

81
00:08:54.073 --> 00:08:59.064
barriers in places, tasks, routines and
expectations, and ask what could change

82
00:08:59.065 --> 00:09:03.865
around the student. Picture a crowded
corridor between periods. There may be

83
00:09:03.866 --> 00:09:08.665
noise, unpredictable movement, people
brushing past, and very little time to

84
00:09:08.666 --> 00:09:13.850
reach the next room. Before deciding that
the student needs to cope better, we can

85
00:09:13.851 --> 00:09:18.907
ask what options exist. Could information
about the route be clearer? Is there a

86
00:09:18.908 --> 00:09:23.579
less crowded route or a different
transition arrangement worth discussing?

87
00:09:23.580 --> 00:09:28.316
Those are possibilities to explore with
the student. We can't know from the

88
00:09:28.317 --> 00:09:32.604
corridor alone which one would help.
Include their communication and

89
00:09:32.605 --> 00:09:37.597
experience, try an appropriate change, and
review whether participation becomes

90
00:09:37.598 --> 00:09:42.526
more workable. Changing an environment
won't remove every difficulty. It gives

91
00:09:42.527 --> 00:09:45.534
us something practical and important to
examine.

92
00:09:47.040 --> 00:09:52.080
Autism can be understood as difference and
dynamic disability. Access and

93
00:09:52.081 --> 00:09:57.471
support needs can change with the task,
the environment, relationships and the

94
00:09:57.472 --> 00:10:02.862
capacity available at that moment. In
secondary school, that may happen within

95
00:10:02.863 --> 00:10:08.603
a single day. A student might participate
comfortably in one subject, then find the

96
00:10:08.604 --> 00:10:13.783
next period much harder. The room,
teacher, instructions, pace and previous

97
00:10:13.784 --> 00:10:19.244
demands may all be different. Managing
something yesterday doesn't tell us what

98
00:10:19.245 --> 00:10:24.565
support is needed today. So we keep our
assumptions flexible. We ask what has

99
00:10:24.566 --> 00:10:30.096
changed, what the task requires, and what
the student can tell us. Strengths and

100
00:10:30.097 --> 00:10:35.837
substantial support needs can both belong
in the picture. Support should respond to

101
00:10:35.838 --> 00:10:41.228
the person and the situation, rather than
to a fixed idea of what that student

102
00:10:41.229 --> 00:10:42.348
ought to manage.

103
00:10:43.880 --> 00:10:48.704
Many of us learnt about five senses:
touch, vision, taste, hearing and smell.

104
00:10:48.705 --> 00:10:53.401
This workshop extends that picture to
eight. Proprioception is our sense of

105
00:10:53.402 --> 00:10:58.416
where the body is in space. It involves
information from our muscles and joints.

106
00:10:58.417 --> 00:11:03.368
Think about knowing where your feet are as
you move through a busy room, or how

107
00:11:03.369 --> 00:11:08.065
much pressure to use when carrying
equipment. The vestibular sense concerns

108
00:11:08.066 --> 00:11:13.080
balance and movement. Moving on stairs,
changing direction in a game, rocking in

109
00:11:13.081 --> 00:11:17.587
a chair, or being still after movement can
involve this sense. Different

110
00:11:17.588 --> 00:11:22.348
students may seek or avoid different kinds
of movement. Interoception is our

111
00:11:22.349 --> 00:11:26.982
sense of signals from inside the body.
We'll return to it shortly, when we

112
00:11:26.983 --> 00:11:31.363
consider hunger, thirst and feelings.
Across a school day, senses work

113
00:11:31.364 --> 00:11:35.933
together. A science laboratory, the
canteen, a music room and the bus bay

114
00:11:35.934 --> 00:11:40.758
offer quite different experiences. Keep
the question open: what is this place

115
00:11:40.759 --> 00:11:43.043
like for this student, at this time?

116
00:11:44.560 --> 00:11:49.411
Sensory experience includes pleasure,
connection and regulation, as well as

117
00:11:49.412 --> 00:11:54.197
discomfort or overload. We need both parts
in view. Notice sensory joy. It

118
00:11:54.198 --> 00:11:59.443
might be a sound, a texture, a movement or
another experience the student values.

119
00:11:59.444 --> 00:12:04.295
Stimming can also be part of pleasure,
regulation and authentic expression.

120
00:12:04.296 --> 00:12:09.016
Harmless stimming does not need to be
discouraged simply because it looks

121
00:12:09.017 --> 00:12:13.802
different. Think about where you see
enjoyment in the students you have in

122
00:12:13.803 --> 00:12:19.047
mind. If you aren't sure what an action
means for someone, that's a reason to ask

123
00:12:19.048 --> 00:12:24.162
in a way they can use. Avoid treating
difference as disruption by default. What

124
00:12:24.163 --> 00:12:28.817
would the student want other people to
understand about that experience?

125
00:12:30.320 --> 00:12:34.945
This slide introduces detail-first
thinking, sometimes described as

126
00:12:34.946 --> 00:12:40.482
bottom-up processing. The image builds a
whole scene from individual details. It

127
00:12:40.483 --> 00:12:46.090
offers a way to think about how someone
may notice specifics and build the bigger

128
00:12:46.091 --> 00:12:51.136
picture from them. That can help us notice
genuine strengths: recognising

129
00:12:51.137 --> 00:12:55.272
patterns, remembering detail,
understanding systems, finding

130
00:12:55.273 --> 00:12:59.968
inconsistencies, or engaging with a
subject very deeply. Honesty and

131
00:12:59.969 --> 00:13:05.575
directness, logical precision and depth of
connection may also be important parts

132
00:13:05.576 --> 00:13:10.832
of a student's experience. These are
possibilities to notice in individuals,

133
00:13:10.833 --> 00:13:16.159
rather than a checklist every Autistic
student must match. A fuller intake of

134
00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:21.696
detail can also create load. Something
that seems like background information to

135
00:13:21.697 --> 00:13:26.813
one person may be very noticeable to
another. We can value precision while

136
00:13:26.814 --> 00:13:32.280
considering what the environment asks of
the student. On page five, Strengths I

137
00:13:32.281 --> 00:13:37.537
can see, choose one strength you've
actually observed in each student. Where

138
00:13:37.538 --> 00:13:41.882
or when does it show up? Pause if you'd
like time to record it.

139
00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:54.620
As you review those notes, check that the
strength is described in its own right.

140
00:13:54.621 --> 00:13:58.792
It is something present now that an
educator can recognise and build on.

141
00:13:58.793 --> 00:14:03.610
Where you don't yet have an example, keep
that space open for further listening and

142
00:14:03.611 --> 00:14:04.315
observation.

143
00:14:05.840 --> 00:14:10.862
Now consider where attention goes. The
torch and floodlight on the slide show

144
00:14:10.863 --> 00:14:15.554
two ways attention can spread. A
floodlight covers a wider area. A torch

145
00:14:15.555 --> 00:14:20.444
concentrates light more deeply in one
place. Monotropism is a framework for

146
00:14:20.445 --> 00:14:25.335
understanding attention that channels into
fewer things with greater depth.

147
00:14:25.336 --> 00:14:30.291
Developed by Dinah Murray, Mike Lesser and
Wenn Lawson, it helps us consider

148
00:14:30.292 --> 00:14:34.984
deep interests, transitions and the
experience of flow. It is a lens for

149
00:14:34.985 --> 00:14:39.544
inquiry, with individual differences still
in view. Think of a student

150
00:14:39.545 --> 00:14:44.368
absorbed in coding, a design project,
writing, or a topic they love. Their

151
00:14:44.369 --> 00:14:49.523
attention is invested there. When the bell
goes, stopping and moving to another

152
00:14:49.524 --> 00:14:54.678
subject can ask a great deal. A warning, a
clear place to stop, and support for

153
00:14:54.679 --> 00:14:59.568
the transition may make that shift more
manageable. Deep interests can give

154
00:14:59.569 --> 00:15:04.855
access to learning, memory and joy. Full
absorption can be a valuable experience.

155
00:15:04.856 --> 00:15:09.614
Alongside helping students change
activities when needed, we can look for

156
00:15:09.615 --> 00:15:12.851
opportunities to protect that depth of
engagement.

157
00:15:14.360 --> 00:15:19.577
Executive functioning gives us language
for the demands within a task. Planning

158
00:15:19.578 --> 00:15:24.662
is working out the steps. Organisation
concerns the materials and information

159
00:15:24.663 --> 00:15:29.814
needed. Time management includes judging
and using the time available. Working

160
00:15:29.815 --> 00:15:35.032
memory helps us hold information while we
use it. Meta-cognition means thinking

161
00:15:35.033 --> 00:15:40.184
about our own thinking. The other areas on
the slide are emotional regulation,

162
00:15:40.185 --> 00:15:45.335
flexibility, attention, task initiation
and persistence. They include managing

163
00:15:45.336 --> 00:15:50.019
feelings, adapting when something changes,
directing attention, getting

164
00:15:50.020 --> 00:15:55.036
started and continuing with a task.
Consider an assessment handed out in the

165
00:15:55.037 --> 00:16:00.255
last period. The subject knowledge is only
part of what it asks. A student also

166
00:16:00.256 --> 00:16:05.072
needs to understand the instructions,
organise materials, decide where to

167
00:16:05.073 --> 00:16:10.424
begin, plan work over time, and remember
how to submit it. These terms are lenses

168
00:16:10.425 --> 00:16:15.375
for inquiry. They don't tell us why a
particular student is struggling. Ask

169
00:16:15.376 --> 00:16:19.991
which demand needs support. Written steps,
a visible first action or a

170
00:16:19.992 --> 00:16:24.608
clearer schedule may be useful
possibilities to investigate, depending

171
00:16:24.609 --> 00:16:26.480
on the student and the task.

172
00:16:28.000 --> 00:16:32.444
Let's return to interoception, our
awareness and understanding of internal

173
00:16:32.445 --> 00:16:37.193
body signals. Those signals can include
temperature, hunger, thirst and needing

174
00:16:37.194 --> 00:16:42.125
the bathroom. They help us make sense of
how we're doing inside. Alexithymia means

175
00:16:42.126 --> 00:16:46.631
difficulty identifying and describing
emotions and feelings. A person might

176
00:16:46.632 --> 00:16:51.320
find it hard to distinguish excitement
from anxiety, or to find words for what

177
00:16:51.321 --> 00:16:55.886
they feel. The questions on the slide
remind us that an outward sign doesn't

178
00:16:55.887 --> 00:17:00.453
always tell us the inner experience.
Crying, for example, does not give us a

179
00:17:00.454 --> 00:17:04.837
complete explanation of someone's
feelings. In school, we might ask how a

180
00:17:04.838 --> 00:17:09.708
student can communicate a need or access a
break when a signal is unclear or hard

181
00:17:09.709 --> 00:17:14.458
to describe. We shouldn't assume that
appearing calm means feeling comfortable,

182
00:17:14.459 --> 00:17:19.207
or that a student can always explain a
feeling on demand. Keep listening, allow

183
00:17:19.208 --> 00:17:22.008
time and stay curious about what we cannot
see.

184
00:17:23.520 --> 00:17:28.864
We're going to put that environment-first
lens to work. On page five of your guide,

185
00:17:28.865 --> 00:17:33.948
choose one familiar routine in a class or
across the school day. Moving between

186
00:17:33.949 --> 00:17:39.032
periods, entering a practical science
lesson, changing for P E, or attending an

187
00:17:39.033 --> 00:17:43.725
assembly could be useful starting points.
First, name the routine without

188
00:17:43.726 --> 00:17:48.744
identifying a student or school. Then list
the sensory and task demands before

189
00:17:48.745 --> 00:17:53.894
listing any student responses. What is the
environment asking someone to manage?

190
00:17:53.895 --> 00:17:58.913
Next, add possible options that might
increase access or choice. Include how a

191
00:17:58.914 --> 00:18:03.997
student could communicate what helps.
Finally, turn each option into a question

192
00:18:03.998 --> 00:18:09.277
to explore. For example, could a clearer
transition arrangement help here, and how

193
00:18:09.278 --> 00:18:14.426
would we find out with the student? Work
privately, or pause and talk it through

194
00:18:14.427 --> 00:18:19.380
with a colleague if you're watching
together. We are reviewing a routine, not

195
00:18:19.381 --> 00:18:24.334
trying to simulate another person's
sensory experience. One grounded question

196
00:18:24.335 --> 00:18:29.092
is enough. Pause the video for a few
minutes, then return for the debrief.

197
00:18:37.120 --> 00:18:42.333
Look back over your notes. Have you
described demands in the environment and

198
00:18:42.334 --> 00:18:47.965
task, or have you slipped into describing
a student as the problem? If you have an

199
00:18:47.966 --> 00:18:53.388
option in mind, whose knowledge will help
you decide whether it fits? Lighting,

200
00:18:53.389 --> 00:18:58.881
sound, movement, proximity and materials
may all matter differently to different

201
00:18:58.882 --> 00:19:04.165
students. An option is still a question
until you explore it in context. What

202
00:19:04.166 --> 00:19:09.170
could educators change, offer or
investigate before increasing demands on

203
00:19:09.171 --> 00:19:13.620
the student? Keep that question with you
as you return to school.

204
00:19:15.160 --> 00:19:19.941
Before Session Two, there is a light
noticing task on page six. Choose one

205
00:19:19.942 --> 00:19:25.116
familiar routine you're already part of.
Notice what supports access, connection

206
00:19:25.117 --> 00:19:30.161
or joy for the students you have in mind.
Notice what seems to add demand, and

207
00:19:30.162 --> 00:19:35.401
when. Then write down what you don't know
yet as a question to explore. You don't

208
00:19:35.402 --> 00:19:40.380
need to set up an extra activity or
collect identifying information. Keep the

209
00:19:40.381 --> 00:19:45.621
notes brief and private. If you don't get
a chance to do this, you can still take

210
00:19:45.622 --> 00:19:50.403
part fully in the next session. We'll use
those observations as we explore

211
00:19:50.404 --> 00:19:55.447
communication, connection and how demands
build across a secondary school day,

212
00:19:55.448 --> 00:20:00.688
across subjects and teachers. Keep your
guide handy, and come back to Session Two

213
00:20:00.689 --> 00:20:05.601
when you're ready. Thank you for the
attention and thought you've brought to

214
00:20:05.602 --> 00:20:06.846
this first session.

215
00:20:14.880 --> 00:20:19.283
Welcome back to Secondary Education
Online. This is Session Two,

216
00:20:19.284 --> 00:20:24.317
Communication, Connection and Daily
Capacity. Have your Participant Guide

217
00:20:24.318 --> 00:20:29.769
nearby and bring back the two or three
students you had in mind in Session One.

218
00:20:29.770 --> 00:20:35.222
If you're joining the series here, you can
choose students now. Use initials or

219
00:20:35.223 --> 00:20:40.675
Student A, B and C, and keep identifying
details out of anything you share. The

220
00:20:40.676 --> 00:20:45.778
choices for taking part remain open. Quiet
listening and private notes are

221
00:20:45.779 --> 00:20:51.021
welcome. If you're with a live online
group, its chat can be another option.

222
00:20:51.022 --> 00:20:56.544
Cameras are optional, and you can pause,
move or step away whenever you need to.

223
00:20:56.545 --> 00:21:01.927
Since the last session, what have you
noticed about a student, a class, or one

224
00:21:01.928 --> 00:21:07.660
part of the day? You may have a note from
page six, or simply a question. It's fine

225
00:21:07.661 --> 00:21:12.973
if nothing new has emerged. Take a moment
to reconnect with your own starting

226
00:21:12.974 --> 00:21:13.392
point.

227
00:21:21.400 --> 00:21:26.966
Today we'll explore different ways of
communicating and connecting. Then we'll

228
00:21:26.967 --> 00:21:32.678
map a school day across the timetable,
consider what may be hidden by the effort

229
00:21:32.679 --> 00:21:38.173
to appear fine, and choose one focus to
carry into design. Page seven of your

230
00:21:38.174 --> 00:21:43.813
guide holds the key ideas. What any one
teacher sees in a single period is only

231
00:21:43.814 --> 00:21:45.548
part of a student's day.

232
00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:51.873
Let's begin with an ordinary question.
Since you woke up today, how have you

233
00:21:51.874 --> 00:21:56.538
communicated? See if you can think of
three different ways. Perhaps you've

234
00:21:56.539 --> 00:22:01.332
spoken, sent a message, pointed to
something, shown a photo, moved closer or

235
00:22:01.333 --> 00:22:05.934
moved away. You may have used a gesture, a
communication device, a facial

236
00:22:05.935 --> 00:22:10.600
expression, or taken time before
responding. Take a brief moment to notice

237
00:22:10.601 --> 00:22:15.649
the variety in your own morning. You can
keep it to yourself, or add one example

238
00:22:15.650 --> 00:22:17.311
to your live group's chat.

239
00:22:25.320 --> 00:22:29.669
We move between communication methods
throughout the day, often without

240
00:22:29.670 --> 00:22:34.142
noticing. The online setting makes some of
those choices visible: typing,

241
00:22:34.143 --> 00:22:38.368
speaking, reacting, or waiting before
replying. Speech is one part of

242
00:22:38.369 --> 00:22:43.028
communication. In school, we need to
recognise the ways a student is already

243
00:22:43.029 --> 00:22:47.563
communicating and make sure those ways are
available where they need them.

244
00:22:49.080 --> 00:22:54.446
Understanding and expressing are related,
but they don't necessarily look the same.

245
00:22:54.447 --> 00:22:59.617
Understanding concerns information coming
in and how a person makes sense of it.

246
00:22:59.618 --> 00:23:04.853
Expressing concerns messages going out,
through any communication method. Someone

247
00:23:04.854 --> 00:23:09.827
may speak fluently and still need support
to understand rapid instructions or

248
00:23:09.828 --> 00:23:14.998
unfamiliar subject language. Someone else
may understand much more than they can

249
00:23:14.999 --> 00:23:19.907
readily express in speech. How much
someone speaks does not tell us how much

250
00:23:19.908 --> 00:23:23.900
they understand. A A C stands for
augmentative and alternative

251
00:23:23.901 --> 00:23:29.071
communication. It can include signs, a
communication board or book, typing, or a

252
00:23:29.072 --> 00:23:34.307
communication device. Some people use it
alongside speech and some use it instead

253
00:23:34.308 --> 00:23:39.608
of speech. Communication can also change
with the setting, demands and how someone

254
00:23:39.609 --> 00:23:44.648
is feeling. Think privately about your
room and your subject. How can students

255
00:23:44.649 --> 00:23:49.950
receive information, ask a question, make
a choice or show you what they know? Our

256
00:23:49.951 --> 00:23:54.924
role here is to improve access and work
with the student, family and relevant

257
00:23:54.925 --> 00:23:58.786
team. Individual assessment questions
belong with that team.

258
00:24:00.320 --> 00:24:05.335
The next film brings us back to people's
own accounts of communication. As you

259
00:24:05.336 --> 00:24:09.373
watch, notice what supports communication.
Choose one idea that

260
00:24:09.374 --> 00:24:14.715
stands out to you. Let the speakers lead,
and leave room for different experiences.

261
00:29:47.200 --> 00:29:51.605
What stood out to you about what supported
communication? What strengths

262
00:29:51.606 --> 00:29:56.445
did you notice? What did another person do
that helped, and what difference did

263
00:29:56.446 --> 00:30:00.665
the environment or available communication
method make? Take a moment

264
00:30:00.666 --> 00:30:05.195
to hold onto one idea. You can write it
privately or share it in your live

265
00:30:05.196 --> 00:30:08.793
group's chat if you wish. Pause here for
longer reflection.

266
00:30:16.800 --> 00:30:21.475
Keep that idea as something to explore
with the students you have in mind. The

267
00:30:21.476 --> 00:30:25.908
film offers individual accounts, and we
should avoid turning them into one

268
00:30:25.909 --> 00:30:30.706
communication profile for everyone. One
practical access question travels across

269
00:30:30.707 --> 00:30:35.079
the secondary day: is a student's
communication system available in every

270
00:30:35.080 --> 00:30:39.937
period and with every teacher, including
during transitions? It needs to be where

271
00:30:39.938 --> 00:30:41.272
communication happens.

272
00:30:42.800 --> 00:30:48.199
Double empathy invites us to consider
misunderstanding in both directions. When

273
00:30:48.200 --> 00:30:53.600
people have different experiences and ways
of communicating, understanding each

274
00:30:53.601 --> 00:30:58.377
other can take work from everyone
involved. In a class discussion, for

275
00:30:58.378 --> 00:31:03.570
example, a teacher may expect a quick
spoken answer. A student may need more

276
00:31:03.571 --> 00:31:08.970
processing time, written information, or
another way to respond. If we see only

277
00:31:08.971 --> 00:31:14.162
the missing spoken answer, we may miss the
mismatch between expectations and

278
00:31:14.163 --> 00:31:19.286
access. Ask whose expectations are shaping
the interaction. What could make

279
00:31:19.287 --> 00:31:24.479
connection safer or clearer? Those
questions apply to group work, corridors,

280
00:31:24.480 --> 00:31:29.394
break times, online spaces, and
interactions with different teachers and

281
00:31:29.395 --> 00:31:35.002
peers. We can examine our own part in the
exchange as well as trying to understand

282
00:31:35.003 --> 00:31:39.641
the student's perspective. Connection does
not have to look one way.

283
00:31:41.160 --> 00:31:46.220
Students connect with people in family,
school, community and online settings.

284
00:31:46.221 --> 00:31:51.215
Some enjoy one-to-one conversation. Some
enjoy being alongside another person

285
00:31:51.216 --> 00:31:56.473
quietly. Shared interests, gaming, clubs,
sport or music can offer important ways

286
00:31:56.474 --> 00:32:01.534
to connect. Contact may be frequent or
occasional and still be valued. Culture

287
00:32:01.535 --> 00:32:06.858
and individual preference also shape how
people interact. We shouldn't assume that

288
00:32:06.859 --> 00:32:11.788
the connection we can see at school
represents a young person's whole social

289
00:32:11.789 --> 00:32:16.586
world. For many adolescents, online
communities and shared-interest groups

290
00:32:16.587 --> 00:32:21.844
are meaningful places of belonging. Keep
privacy in mind as you reflect. We don't

291
00:32:21.845 --> 00:32:26.313
need details about a student's online
activity, identity or community

292
00:32:26.314 --> 00:32:31.243
membership. The useful question is how
each student connects, and what makes

293
00:32:31.244 --> 00:32:33.543
that connection accessible to them.

294
00:32:35.080 --> 00:32:40.318
Turn to the Connection discussion on page
eight. Consider the students you have in

295
00:32:40.319 --> 00:32:44.846
mind. Where do they spend recess and
lunch, and who with? What kinds of

296
00:32:44.847 --> 00:32:49.957
connection does each of them enjoy? Which
parts of the day are socially hardest:

297
00:32:49.958 --> 00:32:54.355
perhaps group work, corridors, the canteen
or P E? Then focus on what

298
00:32:54.356 --> 00:32:59.465
teachers could change to make connection
easier without putting a student on the

299
00:32:59.466 --> 00:33:03.993
spot. You can think through these
questions quietly. If you're watching

300
00:33:03.994 --> 00:33:08.715
with colleagues, pause for a brief
conversation if you choose. There is no

301
00:33:08.716 --> 00:33:13.437
need to share private details about any
student. If you don't know where a

302
00:33:13.438 --> 00:33:18.418
student goes or what they enjoy, write
that as a question. Spending time alone

303
00:33:18.419 --> 00:33:23.464
may be a choice someone values. Pause the
video and take a few minutes with the

304
00:33:23.465 --> 00:33:23.980
prompts.

305
00:33:32.000 --> 00:33:36.935
As you return, look at the change you've
considered. Does it offer a choice, or

306
00:33:36.935 --> 00:33:41.996
does it assume a student ought to connect
in a particular way? Possible questions

307
00:33:41.996 --> 00:33:46.678
might concern giving group roles in
advance, offering a choice of partners,

308
00:33:46.678 --> 00:33:51.676
avoiding unexpected public requests to
speak, or making a quiet lunchtime option

309
00:33:51.676 --> 00:33:56.295
available. These are examples to
investigate with a student. We need their

310
00:33:56.295 --> 00:34:01.040
perspective to understand whether an
option feels helpful. Keep one question

311
00:34:01.040 --> 00:34:03.253
that could guide that conversation.

312
00:34:04.761 --> 00:34:09.569
The battery on this slide gives us a
simple way to talk about energy and

313
00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:14.379
capacity. A day can begin with a different
amount of charge. Actions use

314
00:34:14.380 --> 00:34:19.730
energy, and when the battery runs low, it
may show or it may remain hidden. Some

315
00:34:19.731 --> 00:34:24.607
people describe this through spoons. There
is a limited supply, different

316
00:34:24.608 --> 00:34:29.619
actions use some of them, and low spoons
can make further demands harder to

317
00:34:29.620 --> 00:34:34.564
manage. Both images are metaphors that can
open a conversation. They don't

318
00:34:34.565 --> 00:34:39.576
measure a student's internal state. The
right support can also restore some

319
00:34:39.577 --> 00:34:44.724
capacity. A valued activity, a workable
break, clearer information or a safer

320
00:34:44.725 --> 00:34:49.669
connection may make a difference. What
helps will vary. As we map the day,

321
00:34:49.670 --> 00:34:53.800
include what gives something back as well
as what uses energy.

322
00:34:55.321 --> 00:35:00.162
Six classrooms, six teachers, one student.
Take a moment to picture one

323
00:35:00.163 --> 00:35:05.835
student's route through the day. How many
rooms, adults, corridor trips and changes

324
00:35:05.836 --> 00:35:10.816
of expectation does it involve? Each
period can bring a different sensory

325
00:35:10.817 --> 00:35:16.073
setting, subject language and way of
organising a task. Each teacher sees one

326
00:35:16.074 --> 00:35:21.261
part of that experience, with limited
information about what happened before

327
00:35:21.262 --> 00:35:26.934
the student arrived. Something helpful in
period one may be unknown in period four.

328
00:35:26.935 --> 00:35:32.122
Sharing what works is something a school
can plan for. It needn't mean every

329
00:35:32.123 --> 00:35:37.587
teacher discovering the same information
separately. Next session we'll consider

330
00:35:37.588 --> 00:35:43.121
supports that travel, including concise
guidance written with the student. First,

331
00:35:43.122 --> 00:35:48.586
let's put more of the day in one place so
we can see what we may otherwise miss.

332
00:35:50.120 --> 00:35:55.186
Open the school-day map on page nine. You
can use a familiar timetable or the

333
00:35:55.186 --> 00:36:00.119
sample Year 9 Thursday on page ten. The
sample is there if you don't know a

334
00:36:00.119 --> 00:36:05.385
student's whole timetable or would prefer
to avoid using real details. Work from

335
00:36:05.385 --> 00:36:10.651
arrival through home group, each period,
breaks and departure. In each row, note

336
00:36:10.651 --> 00:36:15.917
the room, the teacher and what happens.
Then, for each student you have in mind,

337
00:36:15.917 --> 00:36:21.250
use C for something that may cost energy,
L for something that may lighten demand

338
00:36:21.250 --> 00:36:26.450
or give energy back, and a question mark
where you don't know yet. You can mark

339
00:36:26.450 --> 00:36:31.449
both C and L in the same period. The
sample day includes a relief teacher in

340
00:36:31.449 --> 00:36:36.049
science, an unexpected maths quiz,
changing for P E, group work and an

341
00:36:36.049 --> 00:36:41.315
assessment handed out at the end of the
day. Use those events as questions about

342
00:36:41.315 --> 00:36:46.248
demand. We can't assume their meaning for
every student. Map the whole day,

343
00:36:46.248 --> 00:36:51.247
including corridors, lockers, breaks and
the journey out. Work privately, or

344
00:36:51.247 --> 00:36:56.580
compare general patterns with a colleague
if you choose. Keep the student details

345
00:36:56.580 --> 00:37:01.311
to yourself. Pause here for about seven
minutes, or as long as you need.

346
00:37:09.321 --> 00:37:13.711
Let that first map be a working picture.
It doesn't have to be complete. A

347
00:37:13.712 --> 00:37:18.522
question mark is useful because it shows
where student, family or staff knowledge

348
00:37:18.523 --> 00:37:23.153
could add something. You may also have
noticed how much of the day sits beyond

349
00:37:23.154 --> 00:37:27.662
your own lesson. Keep the map open,
because the next step adds detail to it.

350
00:37:29.201 --> 00:37:34.427
Page eleven asks, What costs? What
lightens? Return to the marks you've just

351
00:37:34.428 --> 00:37:39.445
made. For a cost, name the demand or
uncertainty more precisely. It might

352
00:37:39.446 --> 00:37:44.811
involve a change announced only once,
unclear group expectations, or having to

353
00:37:44.812 --> 00:37:50.526
move quickly through a crowded place. For
a lightener, note what may reduce demand,

354
00:37:50.527 --> 00:37:55.962
restore capacity or bring joy. Include
safe connection and interests as well as

355
00:37:55.963 --> 00:38:00.980
formal support. Where the student's
experience is unknown, add a question

356
00:38:00.981 --> 00:38:06.416
instead of an explanation. Something can
be enjoyable or successfully completed

357
00:38:06.417 --> 00:38:11.991
and still take considerable effort. Pause
for a few minutes to add to the map. If

358
00:38:11.992 --> 00:38:17.567
you're with a live group, you may share
one general pattern under costs, lightens

359
00:38:17.568 --> 00:38:20.422
or questions, without identifying anyone.

360
00:38:28.441 --> 00:38:33.323
Now look across the page. What builds up?
Where might there be an opportunity for

361
00:38:33.324 --> 00:38:37.901
support or recovery? The pattern may
differ between the students you have in

362
00:38:37.902 --> 00:38:42.601
mind, and it may change for the same
student across days. Keep the distinction

363
00:38:42.602 --> 00:38:47.545
between what you've observed and what you
still need to ask. That distinction will

364
00:38:47.546 --> 00:38:49.801
help us design a thoughtful response.

365
00:38:51.321 --> 00:38:56.730
The stack on this slide is a visual
reminder of accumulation. The darker bars

366
00:38:56.731 --> 00:39:01.998
represent demand. The lighter bars
represent relief and recovery. We aren't

367
00:39:01.999 --> 00:39:06.980
assigning scores to students or
calculating their energy. A moment that

368
00:39:06.981 --> 00:39:12.746
seems small in isolation may arrive after
many earlier demands. By the same token,

369
00:39:12.747 --> 00:39:18.370
an earlier support or valued experience
may matter later. No single teacher sees

370
00:39:18.371 --> 00:39:23.780
the whole stack. Glance back at your map
and notice where further information

371
00:39:23.781 --> 00:39:28.192
could change your understanding of a
moment in your own lesson.

372
00:39:29.721 --> 00:39:34.763
Masking concerns the effort someone may
make to hide or manage parts of

373
00:39:34.764 --> 00:39:40.598
themselves in order to fit expectations.
Looking only at outward behaviour or task

374
00:39:40.599 --> 00:39:46.433
completion can leave a great deal unseen.
Keep these questions private. What might

375
00:39:46.434 --> 00:39:52.052
a student be working hard to manage or
hide? Where can they feel recognised and

376
00:39:52.053 --> 00:39:57.672
included? What supports belonging while
allowing them to be themselves? What do

377
00:39:57.673 --> 00:40:02.786
they want others to understand? We can't
determine masking from a single

378
00:40:02.787 --> 00:40:08.117
behaviour, and visible quietness doesn't
give us the whole story. Alongside

379
00:40:08.118 --> 00:40:13.592
demands, keep strengths, joy and safe
connection in view. The next few slides

380
00:40:13.593 --> 00:40:19.428
touch on burnout and School Can't. You're
welcome to pause, look away or step away

381
00:40:19.429 --> 00:40:21.731
and return whenever you need to.

382
00:40:23.241 --> 00:40:28.724
The pressure to fit in can be especially
strong in the secondary years. Masking

383
00:40:28.725 --> 00:40:33.856
may increase as belonging with peers
matters more. Working out who you are

384
00:40:33.857 --> 00:40:39.410
while also hiding parts of yourself can
take a great deal of energy. Burnout and

385
00:40:39.411 --> 00:40:44.964
School Can't can build over time. We will
return to partnership with families in

386
00:40:44.965 --> 00:40:49.956
Session Four, including how home and
school may see different parts of a

387
00:40:49.957 --> 00:40:55.158
student's capacity. Here, the point is to
notice the demands without blame.

388
00:40:55.159 --> 00:41:00.642
Adolescence is also a time of growth.
Young people can build self-understanding

389
00:41:00.643 --> 00:41:06.126
and practise communicating what they need
when school feels safe. A student may

390
00:41:06.127 --> 00:41:11.610
need time, preparation and different ways
to express a view. Feeling safe to be

391
00:41:11.611 --> 00:41:17.023
themselves helps make that possible. We
don't need personal stories in a group

392
00:41:17.024 --> 00:41:21.381
chat to consider what a school could do to
support that safety.

393
00:41:22.921 --> 00:41:27.830
Good and chilled in class. It's a familiar
description, and it's worth

394
00:41:27.831 --> 00:41:33.168
looking beneath it. A quiet student may be
using a great deal of effort that

395
00:41:33.169 --> 00:41:38.220
other people don't see. Calm does not
always mean okay. Sometimes it may

396
00:41:38.221 --> 00:41:43.914
involve masking. Think privately about a
student whose needs are easy to overlook

397
00:41:43.915 --> 00:41:49.393
because they rarely interrupt or ask for
help. What do you actually know about

398
00:41:49.394 --> 00:41:54.375
their experience, and what have you
assumed? There is no need to answer

399
00:41:54.376 --> 00:41:59.142
aloud. Hold the question gently as we move
into the next reflection.

400
00:42:02.161 --> 00:42:06.730
On page twelve, consider the three masking
discussion questions. Which

401
00:42:06.731 --> 00:42:12.028
classes, teachers or times of day might
ask the most of a student to appear fine?

402
00:42:12.029 --> 00:42:17.326
Where at school do they seem most able to
be themselves? Who would notice if they

403
00:42:17.327 --> 00:42:22.293
were struggling, and how would that
information reach the right people? Keep

404
00:42:22.294 --> 00:42:26.862
the first two questions private. If you're
with a live group, the last

405
00:42:26.863 --> 00:42:31.829
question can be discussed as a school
process: how information moves between

406
00:42:31.830 --> 00:42:36.597
subject teachers, year level staff,
learning support, wellbeing staff and

407
00:42:36.598 --> 00:42:41.498
home. Avoid naming a student or an
individual staff member. Pause the video

408
00:42:41.499 --> 00:42:43.816
for a couple of minutes to reflect.

409
00:42:51.841 --> 00:42:57.097
The question about who notices is also a
question about how we listen. Information

410
00:42:57.098 --> 00:43:01.706
may come through a student's own
communication, a family conversation or

411
00:43:01.707 --> 00:43:06.898
something a colleague observes. A useful
process allows that information to reach

412
00:43:06.899 --> 00:43:11.571
the people who can respond, with privacy
respected. If your notes contain

413
00:43:11.572 --> 00:43:16.569
uncertainty, keep it as a prompt for
consultation. It doesn't need to become a

414
00:43:16.570 --> 00:43:19.100
claim about someone's inner experience.

415
00:43:20.601 --> 00:43:25.690
Let's choose a clear focus to carry into
Session Three. On page thirteen, complete

416
00:43:25.691 --> 00:43:30.090
Keep, Reduce and Explore. Keep one
practice because it supports access,

417
00:43:30.091 --> 00:43:34.929
connection or joy. Reduce one barrier or
unnecessary demand. Tick whether that

418
00:43:34.930 --> 00:43:39.768
focus is a lesson, an assessment task or a
handover. Explore one question with

419
00:43:39.769 --> 00:43:44.733
students, families or the team. You don't
need to redesign the whole day. Choose

420
00:43:44.734 --> 00:43:49.886
something specific enough to work with in
the next session. Keep the day map nearby

421
00:43:49.887 --> 00:43:54.914
as your evidence, including the parts you
still need to understand. Pause here to

422
00:43:54.915 --> 00:43:56.358
write your three notes.

423
00:44:04.361 --> 00:44:09.789
Check that your Reduce item describes
something in the environment, task or

424
00:44:09.790 --> 00:44:15.659
routine that educators can examine. Make
sure the Keep item protects what already

425
00:44:15.660 --> 00:44:21.161
works. Your question may be just as
important as your proposed change. Bring

426
00:44:21.162 --> 00:44:25.490
all three, and the map behind them, into
the design session.

427
00:44:27.001 --> 00:44:31.571
Before we close, let the room settle. You
can look away from the screen for a

428
00:44:31.572 --> 00:44:36.142
moment if that helps. Across these first
two sessions, you've been building a

429
00:44:36.143 --> 00:44:40.652
fuller picture of the students you have in
mind. Sensory experience, ways of

430
00:44:40.653 --> 00:44:45.343
thinking and attention, internal signals,
communication and connection all form

431
00:44:45.344 --> 00:44:49.914
part of that picture. So do strengths,
interests and moments of joy. Managing

432
00:44:49.915 --> 00:44:54.304
demands across rooms, teachers and
expectations takes resources. Sometimes

433
00:44:54.305 --> 00:44:58.935
there is also the effort of appearing fine
when the inner experience is harder

434
00:44:58.936 --> 00:45:03.566
than it looks. A young person who knows
who they are, and feels valued for it,

435
00:45:03.567 --> 00:45:08.257
has more available for other parts of
life. The research discussed in the guide

436
00:45:08.258 --> 00:45:12.708
connects a positive Autistic identity with
wellbeing. In our everyday work,

437
00:45:12.709 --> 00:45:17.579
that invites a practical question: where
can this student be recognised and valued

438
00:45:17.580 --> 00:45:21.909
as themselves? Real choice matters,
including how they communicate, where

439
00:45:21.910 --> 00:45:26.360
they sit and when they step back.
Connection with other Autistic people can

440
00:45:26.361 --> 00:45:31.171
also be important. Support the students
you teach to know themselves, and to know

441
00:45:31.172 --> 00:45:35.982
that who they are is worth knowing. Thank
you for joining this session. Keep your

442
00:45:35.983 --> 00:45:40.914
map and your Keep, Reduce, Explore notes.
Session Three turns them into a practical

443
00:45:40.915 --> 00:45:43.440
change that you can try, review and
refine.

444
00:45:52.441 --> 00:45:57.697
Welcome to Session Three, Designing for
Access Across Secondary School. Have your

445
00:45:57.698 --> 00:46:02.625
day map and the Keep, Reduce, Explore
notes from Session Two nearby. The key

446
00:46:02.626 --> 00:46:07.422
ideas for today are on page fourteen of
your Participant Guide. We'll work

447
00:46:07.423 --> 00:46:12.482
towards one small change to a lesson, an
assessment task or a handover. You'll

448
00:46:12.483 --> 00:46:17.607
write a brief, consider some options,
check the design against the students you

449
00:46:17.608 --> 00:46:22.798
have in mind, and decide what to try or
consult about next. You can listen, take

450
00:46:22.799 --> 00:46:28.055
private notes, or use your live group's
chat if you're watching together. Cameras

451
00:46:28.056 --> 00:46:32.918
remain optional. Pause or step away
whenever you need to. If you missed the

452
00:46:32.919 --> 00:46:37.452
last session, use the sample Year 9
timetable on page ten, or choose a

453
00:46:37.453 --> 00:46:42.709
lesson, task or handover you know well.
You can still take part. Take a moment to

454
00:46:42.710 --> 00:46:47.966
bring your chosen barrier back to mind.
What, if anything, have you noticed since

455
00:46:47.967 --> 00:46:52.631
last time? Keep any new question with your
notes. It may help refine the

456
00:46:52.632 --> 00:46:53.090
design.

457
00:46:56.121 --> 00:47:01.478
Students come first. Understanding a
student's experience gives us a starting

458
00:47:01.479 --> 00:47:07.118
point for design. It should never reduce
that person to a list of problems. Today

459
00:47:07.119 --> 00:47:12.617
you'll design one change, then check it
against the different students you have

460
00:47:12.618 --> 00:47:18.046
in mind. Use what you know from their
communication, from the day map and from

461
00:47:18.047 --> 00:47:23.475
what others have shared appropriately.
Keep unknowns as questions. Separate an

462
00:47:23.476 --> 00:47:28.762
observation from an interpretation. A task
wasn't started is an observation.

463
00:47:28.763 --> 00:47:33.345
The student didn't care is an
interpretation that may overlook the

464
00:47:33.346 --> 00:47:38.773
demands involved. A useful brief asks what
the task required and what we could

465
00:47:38.774 --> 00:47:44.131
change. Keep strengths and interests in
view as things to build on, alongside

466
00:47:44.132 --> 00:47:46.739
costs the design may need to address.

467
00:47:48.241 --> 00:47:53.130
Keep a concise picture of each student in
view. What do you know about their

468
00:47:53.131 --> 00:47:57.825
strengths, interests and joy? Their
communication, sensory experience and

469
00:47:57.826 --> 00:48:03.107
thinking? What costs, what lightens, and
where they experience belonging? Consider

470
00:48:03.108 --> 00:48:08.388
what the student, family and team already
know, and where a question remains open.

471
00:48:08.389 --> 00:48:13.474
You can jot a private note if that helps,
but this isn't a new file to complete

472
00:48:13.475 --> 00:48:18.495
about one young person. The point is to
hold different students in mind as you

473
00:48:18.496 --> 00:48:23.255
design. Remember the quiet student
described as good and chilled in class.

474
00:48:23.256 --> 00:48:28.276
Looking calm doesn't establish that a
design is working for them. We'll return

475
00:48:28.277 --> 00:48:32.775
to these student perspectives in a private
check later in the session.

476
00:48:34.281 --> 00:48:39.374
Turn to My brief on page fifteen. Choose
one lesson, assessment task or handover

477
00:48:39.375 --> 00:48:43.953
you know well. Your Reduce note from
Session Two may give you a starting

478
00:48:43.954 --> 00:48:48.789
point. Complete the three stems. This asks
students to... For some students,

479
00:48:48.790 --> 00:48:53.561
that means... We could change... Be
specific about the demand. For example,

480
00:48:53.562 --> 00:48:58.205
an assessment may ask students to remember
spoken instructions, plan work

481
00:48:58.206 --> 00:49:02.267
over several weeks and find the submission
steps. That gives you

482
00:49:02.268 --> 00:49:07.168
something concrete to examine. Keep any
explanation of a student's experience

483
00:49:07.169 --> 00:49:12.069
tentative where you still need their view.
Check your wording. Is it workable

484
00:49:12.070 --> 00:49:16.648
and free from blame or deficit labels? If
you've written disorganised or

485
00:49:16.649 --> 00:49:21.549
unmotivated, see whether you can describe
the task and available support more

486
00:49:21.550 --> 00:49:26.256
precisely. Choose something within
educator or school influence. Pause the

487
00:49:26.257 --> 00:49:31.222
video for about five minutes to write your
brief. If you wish, share only your

488
00:49:31.223 --> 00:49:34.187
general We could change line with a live
group.

489
00:49:42.201 --> 00:49:47.122
As you return, read the brief aloud to
yourself. Does it point to an access

490
00:49:47.123 --> 00:49:52.577
question and a change around the student?
Can you tell what information supports it

491
00:49:52.578 --> 00:49:57.699
and what still needs consultation? Keep
this one brief active. We are about to

492
00:49:57.700 --> 00:50:02.353
look at options, and its job is to help
you choose something that fits.

493
00:50:03.881 --> 00:50:08.345
There are four short inputs in this
options menu. The first connects

494
00:50:08.346 --> 00:50:13.211
executive functioning with assessment. The
second considers predictability

495
00:50:13.212 --> 00:50:18.609
across the timetable. The third concerns
supports that travel between teachers and

496
00:50:18.610 --> 00:50:23.475
rooms. The fourth looks at visual
supports. Keep your brief visible as you

497
00:50:23.476 --> 00:50:27.940
listen. Which option might answer the
demand you named? You can note

498
00:50:27.941 --> 00:50:33.139
possibilities in Options I chose on page
fifteen. You will choose one or two to

499
00:50:33.140 --> 00:50:37.270
work with after the inputs. There is no
need to use everything.

500
00:50:38.801 --> 00:50:44.989
Start with assessment. Imagine a capable
student misses a task. Before settling on

501
00:50:44.990 --> 00:50:50.719
motivation as the explanation, ask what
starting, planning and managing time

502
00:50:50.720 --> 00:50:56.832
required. Publishing assessment schedules
early makes information available while

503
00:50:56.833 --> 00:51:02.639
it can still help. Chunking a task with
visible milestones shows intermediate

504
00:51:02.640 --> 00:51:08.369
steps. An exemplar can help students see
the finished thing they are working

505
00:51:08.370 --> 00:51:14.329
towards. Literal submission instructions
clarify exactly where, how and when to

506
00:51:14.330 --> 00:51:20.212
hand it in. If information is hard to hold
in mind, keep it where students can

507
00:51:20.213 --> 00:51:25.714
return to it. Written first steps,
instructions that stay on a class page

508
00:51:25.715 --> 00:51:31.444
and a visible time cue may be useful.
Consider homework too. How much of the

509
00:51:31.445 --> 00:51:37.557
task concerns subject knowledge, and how
much concerns organising and remembering

510
00:51:37.558 --> 00:51:43.287
what to do? Match support to the known
demand and check it with the student.

511
00:51:43.288 --> 00:51:49.400
Where assessment policy sits beyond your
role, note who needs to be involved. The

512
00:51:49.401 --> 00:51:54.977
purpose is to help students access the
task and show what they know, while

513
00:51:54.978 --> 00:51:58.490
retaining its meaningful learning
requirements.

514
00:52:00.001 --> 00:52:05.155
Predictability means having useful
information about routine and change. It

515
00:52:05.156 --> 00:52:09.961
cannot promise that nothing unexpected
will happen. Across a secondary

516
00:52:09.962 --> 00:52:14.977
timetable, a great deal may be spoken
once, implied, or held by adults. A

517
00:52:14.978 --> 00:52:20.550
student arriving to a relief teacher, a
room change and a different task may have

518
00:52:20.551 --> 00:52:25.635
several questions at once. What is
happening? What is changing? What comes

519
00:52:25.636 --> 00:52:31.277
next? What can I do if the plan changes?
Look at your day map. How would a student

520
00:52:31.278 --> 00:52:35.875
find that information if they couldn't
rely on remembering a spoken

521
00:52:35.876 --> 00:52:41.517
announcement? Could they check the class
page or timetable system? Would the usual

522
00:52:41.518 --> 00:52:46.185
start routine continue with a relief
teacher? How could they ask for

523
00:52:46.186 --> 00:52:51.897
clarification in a way they can use? Give
information early enough to matter and in

524
00:52:51.898 --> 00:52:57.191
a form that makes sense to the student.
Clear notice of change can be just as

525
00:52:57.192 --> 00:53:00.463
important as clarity about the ordinary
routine.

526
00:53:02.001 --> 00:53:06.859
A useful support needs to travel across
the timetable. What works with one

527
00:53:06.860 --> 00:53:11.917
teacher can become unavailable if the next
teacher doesn't know about it. One

528
00:53:11.918 --> 00:53:16.709
option is a concise profile written with
the student, covering strengths,

529
00:53:16.710 --> 00:53:21.568
interests, what helps and what costs. It
should reflect their voice and be

530
00:53:21.569 --> 00:53:27.026
reviewed with them. Share useful guidance
with the relevant teachers, including new

531
00:53:27.027 --> 00:53:32.018
and relief teachers, while avoiding
unnecessary personal detail. Agree where

532
00:53:32.019 --> 00:53:37.009
the information is kept and who updates
it. Consider whether familiar visual

533
00:53:37.010 --> 00:53:42.001
supports or ways of explaining a task
remain available in different rooms. A

534
00:53:42.002 --> 00:53:47.259
document somewhere on a shared drive is
only useful if the right person can find

535
00:53:47.260 --> 00:53:52.517
and use it when needed. Would the period
four teacher know what helps before the

536
00:53:52.518 --> 00:53:57.709
lesson begins? If your brief concerns a
handover, that question may be a useful

537
00:53:57.710 --> 00:54:01.768
test. Keep the student involved in what is
written and shared.

538
00:54:03.281 --> 00:54:07.982
A visual support should clarify something
a student needs to find, understand,

539
00:54:07.983 --> 00:54:12.868
remember or communicate. Start with that
information need. In secondary school, a

540
00:54:12.869 --> 00:54:17.570
support might be written instructions that
remain on a class page, a task map,

541
00:54:17.571 --> 00:54:22.272
a checklist or an assessment calendar. It
should suit the student's age and be

542
00:54:22.273 --> 00:54:26.669
available at the point of use. A visual
format doesn't automatically make

543
00:54:26.670 --> 00:54:30.882
information understandable. The quick
review on page sixteen asks four

544
00:54:30.883 --> 00:54:35.646
questions. Can the student understand it?
Can they access it when they need it?

545
00:54:35.647 --> 00:54:40.103
Does it reflect what is actually
happening, including change? Does it give

546
00:54:40.104 --> 00:54:44.806
useful information or a way to respond? If
a student uses A A C, consider both

547
00:54:44.807 --> 00:54:49.508
access to information and access to their
own communication. Keep their system

548
00:54:49.509 --> 00:54:53.355
available through classes and transitions.
A quiet or non-spoken

549
00:54:53.356 --> 00:54:58.057
response still deserves attention. The
source materials also point to a Visual

550
00:54:58.058 --> 00:55:03.004
Supports Checklist and a deeper webinar.
Their access links remain to be confirmed

551
00:55:03.005 --> 00:55:07.950
by the delivery team. For now, the review
questions in your Participant Guide give

552
00:55:07.951 --> 00:55:09.780
you something concrete to use.

553
00:55:11.321 --> 00:55:16.392
Universal Design for Learning helps us
consider access before a barrier arises.

554
00:55:16.393 --> 00:55:20.880
Make important information clear and
available. Offer workable ways to

555
00:55:20.881 --> 00:55:25.237
participate and respond. Support
engagement while keeping meaningful

556
00:55:25.238 --> 00:55:30.049
challenge. A class might have more than
one way to encounter information or

557
00:55:30.050 --> 00:55:35.252
demonstrate learning, where those choices
fit the learning purpose. What you know

558
00:55:35.253 --> 00:55:40.584
about individual students can inform that
design, and their experience can help you

559
00:55:40.585 --> 00:55:45.787
review it. Having a resource present is
only the beginning. We still need to find

560
00:55:45.788 --> 00:55:47.868
out whether students can use it.

561
00:55:49.401 --> 00:55:54.279
Choose one or two options from the menu
and note them on page fifteen. Add them

562
00:55:54.280 --> 00:55:58.846
to your brief as a draft design. Then open
the Accessibility check on page

563
00:55:58.847 --> 00:56:03.726
sixteen. Can students find and understand
what they need? Can they take part in

564
00:56:03.727 --> 00:56:08.292
more than one way? Can they pause, ask for
help or recover? Is the support

565
00:56:08.293 --> 00:56:13.297
available when demand increases? Run each
question over your draft. If the answer

566
00:56:13.298 --> 00:56:17.613
is not yet, adjust something before moving
on. Think about whether the

567
00:56:17.614 --> 00:56:22.305
support would remain available with
another teacher or on a relief day. This

568
00:56:22.306 --> 00:56:26.934
is an early check, and you'll still need
to test the design through use and

569
00:56:26.935 --> 00:56:30.499
feedback. Pause here while you work
through the questions.

570
00:56:38.521 --> 00:56:43.061
Look at anything you changed. Does it make
access clearer in practice? You

571
00:56:43.062 --> 00:56:47.913
might have added a way to ask a question
privately, made instructions easier to

572
00:56:47.914 --> 00:56:52.765
find, or realised that a support needs to
be available earlier. Keep the design

573
00:56:52.766 --> 00:56:57.493
small enough to try and specific enough to
review. We can now develop it in a

574
00:56:57.494 --> 00:56:58.673
little more detail.

575
00:57:00.201 --> 00:57:04.968
On page seventeen, My design, work through
five steps. State the barrier or

576
00:57:04.969 --> 00:57:09.800
unnecessary demand. Describe the change to
the environment, task or routine.

577
00:57:09.801 --> 00:57:14.890
Explain how students' communication and
experience will be included. Decide what

578
00:57:14.891 --> 00:57:19.787
you will observe. Set a time to review it.
Something a teacher could try this

579
00:57:19.788 --> 00:57:24.813
week is a useful size. Written as well as
spoken instructions, a warning before

580
00:57:24.814 --> 00:57:29.517
packing up, or showing an exemplar earlier
are examples. Your brief should

581
00:57:29.518 --> 00:57:34.349
guide the choice. Consider who else needs
to know, especially if your change

582
00:57:34.350 --> 00:57:39.246
involves an assessment or handover. It may
include subject teachers, learning

583
00:57:39.247 --> 00:57:44.078
support, a year level coordinator or
relief staff. A change beyond your role

584
00:57:44.079 --> 00:57:48.782
can become a question to take to the
appropriate team. Pause the video for

585
00:57:48.783 --> 00:57:53.807
about seven minutes to develop your plan.
If a colleague is available and you'd

586
00:57:53.808 --> 00:57:58.704
like a brief check together, each explain
the change and invite one question.

587
00:57:58.705 --> 00:58:03.923
Otherwise, ask yourself: does the change
address the barrier I named, and would it

588
00:58:03.924 --> 00:58:06.887
hold across subjects, teachers and relief
days?

589
00:58:14.921 --> 00:58:19.994
Bring the design back to one sentence: the
change you propose and the access

590
00:58:19.995 --> 00:58:25.135
question it addresses. Check that your
measure of success tells you something

591
00:58:25.136 --> 00:58:30.412
about participation or experience. Quiet
compliance alone won't tell us whether

592
00:58:30.413 --> 00:58:35.622
access has improved. Your plan can still
contain a question for the student or

593
00:58:35.623 --> 00:58:40.493
family. In fact, it should be open to
their knowledge. Keep that question

594
00:58:40.494 --> 00:58:45.905
beside what you plan to observe and the
date you will review it. We are preparing

595
00:58:45.906 --> 00:58:47.799
something to try and refine.

596
00:58:49.321 --> 00:58:54.066
Now turn to My students check on page
eighteen. Privately, hold the design up

597
00:58:54.067 --> 00:58:58.937
against each of the students you have in
mind. For Student A, B and C, consider

598
00:58:58.938 --> 00:59:03.746
the columns Helps, No difference and Needs
something more. At this stage those

599
00:59:03.747 --> 00:59:08.679
are working judgements to check through
use and consultation. If you don't know,

600
00:59:08.680 --> 00:59:12.801
note the uncertainty rather than guessing.
The same design may work

601
00:59:12.802 --> 00:59:17.797
differently for different students. Keep
names and details private. You can pause

602
00:59:17.798 --> 00:59:22.668
here to think about each person. Notice
where an apparently calm response might

603
00:59:22.669 --> 00:59:25.728
leave you with more to ask about their
experience.

604
00:59:33.761 --> 00:59:38.379
A Needs something more mark gives you
useful information. It shows where a

605
00:59:38.380 --> 00:59:42.683
broader design may need additional
targeted or individual support. No

606
00:59:42.684 --> 00:59:47.682
difference is also worth another look. Was
the original barrier relevant to this

607
00:59:47.683 --> 00:59:52.301
student, and do we know enough about their
experience to judge? This check

608
00:59:52.302 --> 00:59:56.793
keeps real differences visible as we move
into the continuum of support.

609
00:59:58.321 --> 01:00:02.900
Support extends across a continuum. An
inclusive environment reduces barriers

610
01:00:02.901 --> 01:00:07.360
through the ordinary way school is
organised. Clear routines across classes

611
01:00:07.361 --> 01:00:11.759
and an acceptance of different ways to
participate are examples. Universal

612
01:00:11.760 --> 01:00:16.339
supports are available broadly. These
might include information in spoken and

613
01:00:16.340 --> 01:00:21.040
written forms, exemplars and a class page
where instructions remain accessible.

614
01:00:21.041 --> 01:00:25.319
Targeted support responds where additional
assistance is needed, such as

615
01:00:25.320 --> 01:00:29.839
pre-teaching subject vocabulary or
checking in around assessment milestones.

616
01:00:29.840 --> 01:00:33.816
Individual adjustments respond to a
particular student's access and

617
01:00:33.817 --> 01:00:38.035
participation needs. They may involve an
individually agreed assessment

618
01:00:38.036 --> 01:00:42.736
environment, processing time or ways to
show learning, depending on the student

619
01:00:42.737 --> 01:00:46.894
and context. These layers can work
together. A useful universal design

620
01:00:46.895 --> 01:00:51.595
doesn't remove the need for individual
support, and a student shouldn't have to

621
01:00:51.596 --> 01:00:56.115
wait for every broader change before their
needs are addressed. Connect each

622
01:00:56.116 --> 01:01:00.876
response with evidence and consultation,
then review its usefulness through what

623
01:01:00.877 --> 01:01:02.080
happens in practice.

624
01:01:03.601 --> 01:01:08.263
This slide places environmental design and
Universal Design for Learning

